{
  "format": "srwb",
  "version": "1.0",
  "notebook": {
    "name": "Prolog Generates TypR: Compiler Demo",
    "cells": [
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "# Prolog Generates TypR\n\nThis workbook demonstrates UnifyWeaver compiling Prolog predicates into **TypR** — a typed superset of R.\n\nThe workflow:\n1. Define Prolog predicates (facts + rules)\n2. UnifyWeaver detects the recursion pattern (transitive closure)\n3. The compiler generates typed TypR code with `let`, `fn`, and type annotations\n4. Execute the generated TypR code in the TypR kernel\n\n**Requires:** Install the UnifyWeaver package first (Browse Packages).",
        "language": "markdown"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "% Load UnifyWeaver compiler modules\n['../init'].\n:- use_module(unifyweaver(targets/typr_target)).\n:- use_module(unifyweaver(core/recursive_compiler)).",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "load_compiler"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "% Define a family tree as Prolog facts\n:- dynamic parent/2, ancestor/2.\n\nparent(alice, bob).\nparent(bob, charlie).\nparent(bob, diana).\nparent(charlie, eve).\nparent(diana, frank).\n\n% Transitive closure: ancestor(X, Y) if X is an ancestor of Y\nancestor(X, Y) :- parent(X, Y).\nancestor(X, Y) :- parent(X, Z), ancestor(Z, Y).",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "family_tree"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "% Compile ancestor/2 to TypR and write the generated code to a cell\ncompile_recursive(ancestor/2, [target(typr)], Code),\nnb_write('typr_output', '.code', Code).",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "compile_to_typr"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## Generated TypR Code\n\nThe cell below contains the TypR code generated by UnifyWeaver.\nIt includes:\n- Typed function signatures (`fn(start): [#N, char]`)\n- `let` bindings for immutable variables\n- `@{ ... }@` raw-R escape blocks for operations TypR can't express natively yet\n- `character()` for empty vectors (not `c()`)\n- Seeded parent facts from the Prolog assertions\n\nRun it to compute ancestors!",
        "language": "markdown"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "# This cell will be populated by the compiler.\n# Run the 'compile_to_typr' cell above first.",
        "language": "typr",
        "name": "typr_output"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "# Query: all ancestors of alice\nlet results <- ancestor_all(\"alice\");\ncat(paste(\"Descendants of alice:\", paste(results, collapse=\", \")));",
        "language": "typr",
        "name": "query_all"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "# Query: is alice an ancestor of eve?\nlet is_ancestor <- ancestor_check(\"alice\", \"eve\");\ncat(paste(\"alice is ancestor of eve:\", is_ancestor));",
        "language": "typr",
        "name": "query_check"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## Compare: Same Predicate, R Target\n\nUnifyWeaver can compile the same Prolog predicate to plain R.\nThe cell below generates untyped R code from the same `ancestor/2`.",
        "language": "markdown"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "% Compile the same predicate to plain R for comparison\ncompile_recursive(ancestor/2, [target(r)], RCode),\nnb_write('r_output', '.code', RCode).",
        "language": "prolog",
        "name": "compile_to_r"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "# This cell will be populated with plain R code.\n# Run the 'compile_to_r' cell above first.",
        "language": "r",
        "name": "r_output"
      }
    ]
  }
}
